<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Choose two working times</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Two road crews, working together, must finish a job in exactly \({@Td@}\) days. Choose how many days each crew would take working alone. Use whole numbers, and make the two crews different. Many answers are correct.</p><p>Crew 1: [[input:ansa]] days [[validation:ansa]]</p><p>Crew 2: [[input:ansb]] days [[validation:ansb]]</p><p>[[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Crews taking \(a\) and \(b\) days alone do \(\dfrac{1}{a} + \dfrac{1}{b}\) of the job a day together. This must be \(\dfrac{1}{ {@Td@} }\), so \(ab = {@Td@}(a + b)\), which rearranges to \((a - {@Td@})(b - {@Td@}) = {@Td^2@}\).</p><p>So write \({@Td^2@}\) as a product of two different whole numbers and add \({@Td@}\) to each. For example \({@Td^2@} = {@a0-Td@} \times {@b0-Td@}\) gives \(a = {@a0@}\) and \(b = {@b0@}\).</p><p>Check: \(\dfrac{1}{ {@a0@} } + \dfrac{1}{ {@b0@} } = {@fr(1/a0+1/b0)@}\). Other factor pairs of \({@Td^2@}\) give other answers.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
